          "heading": "Why Chimney Cleaning Matters",
          "contentHtml": "<p>Each time you burn wood, the smoke carries byproducts up through the flue. As that smoke cools against the chimney walls, some of it condenses and sticks. The result is soot, a lighter powdery residue, and creosote, a darker deposit that can appear as a tar-like glaze or as brittle flakes. Creosote is flammable, and a heavy buildup is a recognized fire risk in wood-burning systems.</p><p>Buildup also affects how well your fireplace works. A restricted flue can cause weak draft, smoky rooms, and lingering odors. Birds, squirrels, leaves, and fallen debris can add to the problem, especially when the chimney has no cap or the cap is damaged. Regular cleaning keeps the flue clear and gives a trained eye a chance to spot trouble before it grows.</p>"
