      "introHtml": "<p class=\"intro\">Every time you burn wood in your fireplace, the combustion process produces smoke, water vapor, unburned wood particles, and condensed hydrocarbon gases. As these gases travel up the chimney flue and encounter cooler air, they condense along the liner walls into a dark, combustible byproduct known as creosote. However, not all creosote looks or behaves the same way.</p>",
